1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,711
This tutorial follows a cheque from the
drawer's

2
00:00:03,711 --> 00:00:06,958
hand to the bank and back again.

3
00:00:06,958 --> 00:00:09,277
Open Finance and choose Cheques.

4
00:00:09,277 --> 00:00:13,916
Every cheque from every posted contract is
here, with its

5
00:00:13,916 --> 00:00:18,091
status, its maturity date, the property
and the renter.

6
00:00:18,091 --> 00:00:21,338
Use the filters to narrow the list

7
00:00:21,338 --> 00:00:24,121
to registered cheques maturing this month.

8
00:00:24,121 --> 00:00:28,760
Registered means the cheque is recorded
and its journal is

9
00:00:28,760 --> 00:00:32,934
written, but the paper has not left the
office.

10
00:00:32,934 --> 00:00:36,645
Select the rows you are taking to the

11
00:00:36,645 --> 00:00:39,892
bank and choose Cheque and cash
collection.

12
00:00:39,892 --> 00:00:43,139
Enter the deposit date and the account

13
00:00:43,139 --> 00:00:45,922
you are banking into, then confirm.

14
00:00:45,922 --> 00:00:48,706
The selected rows move to deposited.

15
00:00:48,706 --> 00:00:53,344
Depositing is an operational step and
writes no accounting entry,

16
00:00:53,344 --> 00:00:57,519
because nothing has changed about what you
are owed.

17
00:00:57,519 --> 00:01:03,085
When the bank confirms a cheque, open its
row and choose Clear.

18
00:01:03,085 --> 00:01:06,796
Enter the value date the bank gave you.

19
00:01:06,796 --> 00:01:11,435
RentAxis debits your bank account and
credits post-dated cheques receivable.

20
00:01:11,435 --> 00:01:15,146
That is the moment the money becomes
yours.

21
00:01:15,146 --> 00:01:18,857
Now take a cheque the bank has returned.

22
00:01:18,857 --> 00:01:23,959
Open its row and choose Bounce, give the
reason, and confirm.

23
00:01:23,959 --> 00:01:28,598
Because this cheque had already cleared,
RentAxis reverses the bank

24
00:01:28,598 --> 00:01:32,773
side: it debits rent receivable and
credits the bank.

25
00:01:32,773 --> 00:01:38,339
The amount is owed again, and the register
shows it as due.

26
00:01:38,339 --> 00:01:40,195
Open Return and replace.

27
00:01:40,195 --> 00:01:43,442
A returned cheque is usually settled with

28
00:01:43,442 --> 00:01:46,689
new paper, and often more than one.

29
00:01:46,689 --> 00:01:50,400
Add two replacement rows, each with its
own

30
00:01:50,400 --> 00:01:53,647
number, maturity date and amount, then
save.

31
00:01:53,647 --> 00:01:56,430
Each replacement registers its own
journal.

32
00:01:56,430 --> 00:02:01,068
If the replacements do not add up to the
returned

33
00:02:01,068 --> 00:02:05,707
amount, the difference stays in rent
receivable rather than disappearing.

34
00:02:05,707 --> 00:02:10,810
Penalties are separate and never
automatic: a returned cheque proposes a

35
00:02:10,810 --> 00:02:15,912
penalty that finance approves, waives or
reverses from the Penalties queue.
